Before Java 8, methods that might not return a value returned `null`. Callers frequently forgot to null-check and got NPEs.

Optional makes absence **explicit in the type signature**:
```java
// Old: might return null — not obvious from signature
User findUserById(int id); 

// New: clearly communicates "might not exist"
Optional<User> findUserById(int id);
```

Optional forces callers to **acknowledge** the absent case — they can't just use the value directly without handling the empty scenario.

```java
// Empty Optional
Optional<String> empty = Optional.empty();

// Of: throws NPE if value is null
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("hello");

// ofNullable: wraps nullable value
String maybeNull = getValueThatMightBeNull();
Optional<String> safe = Optional.ofNullable(maybeNull);
```

```java
// orElse: default ALWAYS computed (even if Optional has value)
User user = findUser(id).orElse(createDefaultUser()); // createDefaultUser always called!

// orElseGet: Supplier only called if Optional is empty
User user = findUser(id).orElseGet(() -> createDefaultUser()); // lazy

// orElseThrow: throw custom exception if empty
User user = findUser(id)
    .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException("User " + id + " not found"));
```

```java
Optional<User> user = userRepo.findById(id);

// map: transform if present
Optional<String> name = user.map(User::getName);

// Chained map: safe deep access
Optional<String> city = user
    .map(User::getAddress)
    .map(Address::getCity);

// flatMap: when function returns Optional
Optional<Order> order = user
    .flatMap(u -> orderRepo.findByUser(u)); // findByUser returns Optional<Order>
```

**Anti-pattern 1: Optional as parameter**
```java
// BAD: caller must wrap: findUser(Optional.of(id))
void process(Optional<User> user) { }

// GOOD: overloading or @Nullable
void process(User user) { }
void process() { processWithDefault(); }
```

**Anti-pattern 2: Optional as field**
```java
// BAD: not serializable, wastes memory, confusing
class User { Optional<String> middleName; }

// GOOD: just use null and document
class User { @Nullable String middleName; }
```

**Anti-pattern 3: Optional in collections**
```java
// BAD: List<Optional<String>>
// GOOD: List<String> with nulls filtered out, or a filtered stream
```

```java
// Optional.of() — throws NPE if null
String s = "hello";
Optional<String> opt1 = Optional.of(s); // safe
Optional<String> opt2 = Optional.of(null); // NullPointerException!

// Optional.ofNullable() — handles null gracefully
String maybeNull = getFromDatabase(); // might return null
Optional<String> opt3 = Optional.ofNullable(maybeNull);
// If null: Optional.empty()  |  If not: Optional.of(maybeNull)
```

```java
// Old way: manual null checks
User user = userRepo.findById(id); // might return null
if (user != null && user.getAddress() != null) {
    return user.getAddress().getCity();
}
return "Unknown";

// Optional way: clean functional chain
return userRepo.findById(id)  // returns Optional<User>
    .map(User::getAddress)      // Optional<Address>
    .map(Address::getCity)      // Optional<String>
    .orElse("Unknown");         // fallback if any step was empty
```

```java
Optional<User> user = userRepo.findById(id);

// Anti-pattern
if (user.isPresent()) {
    sendEmail(user.get());
}

// Idiomatic: ifPresent
user.ifPresent(this::sendEmail);

// Java 9+: ifPresentOrElse
user.ifPresentOrElse(
    u -> sendEmail(u),
    () -> log.warn("User not found: " + id)
);
```
